---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { EXAMPLES_URL, GUIDES } from "../../lib/guides";
import { breadcrumbs } from "../../lib/jsonld";
import { SITE } from "../../lib/site";

const jsonld = [
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: SITE.name, url: `${SITE.url}/` },
    { name: "Guides", url: `${SITE.url}/guides/` },
  ]),
];
const reviewed = GUIDES.map((g) => g.reviewed).sort().at(-1) ?? "";
---

<Base
  title="Framework guides: verify your API against its spec"
  description="Guides for FastAPI, Express, Hono, Fastify and NestJS: where the spec comes from, how to run apidrifter against the live app, and how to make it automatic."
  jsonld={jsonld}
>
  <h1>Guides</h1>
  <p class="muted">
    One guide per framework. Each says where that framework&rsquo;s OpenAPI document comes
    from, how to run apidrifter against the live app, how to read a real finding and decide
    which side to fix, and how to make the check automatic with an instructions file, the
    Claude Code hook and a CI job. Every guide has a runnable example in the repository with a
    deliberate mismatch, so the findings shown are real output.
  </p>
  <ul class="compare-list">
    {
      GUIDES.map((g) => (
        <li>
          <h2>
            <a href={`/guides/${g.id}/`}>{g.title}</a>
          </h2>
          <p>{g.oneLine}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <h2 id="other-frameworks">Other frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    The loop is the same for anything that speaks HTTP and has an OpenAPI 3 document: start the
    app, run <code>npx apidrifter run &lt;spec&gt; --base-url http://localhost:&lt;port&gt;</code>,
    read the two fixes on each finding, pick a side. The
    <a href="/agents/">agents page</a> has the instructions paragraph and MCP setup that work
    for any codebase; the <a href="/docs/cli/">CLI reference</a> has every flag. The examples
    live at <a href={EXAMPLES_URL} rel="noopener">examples/</a> in the repository.
  </p>
  <p class="reviewed">Last reviewed {reviewed}</p>
</Base>
